### The parent wants to know whether difficult behaviour now predicts harder years ahead.

1 of 71 posts.

This parent, whose toddler's age is unknown, asks what colicky babies and very difficult toddlers become like as teenagers and adults. They describe loving a demanding second toddler while feeling they may be failing to meet her needs. They want hope from others' longer experience rather than an immediate tactic.

- "The colicky babies, the feral toddlers, the sour patch kids... What do they grow up to be like?" [sr065-0065]
- "2nd toddler is a lot. It hasn't been easy. I absolutely love and adore her, but sometimes I feel like I'm failing her, that I'm not what she needs even though I give my best. Yeah, something to unpack in therapy." [sr065-0066]
- "But for now... I guess I'm just looking for ... Hope? What do the teen years look like? And adulthood? Teen years are probably hard haha […] Anyway. Anyone have experiences?" [sr065-0067]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1upebiw

### The parent wants to protect their child from other children's behaviour and decide when to intervene.

2 of 71 posts.

One parent describes friends' children, all under five with exact ages unknown, disrupting meals and influencing the wider group while adults excuse the behaviour. Another describes a two-year-old falling into a tantrum when daycare peers take things, and wants to teach self-advocacy without violence. Both ask how to act within ongoing relationships rather than simply leave them.

- "We all have dinner for example and their kids are throwing food on the ground and stomping on it. Then running around. They actively go to other kids plates and mess with their food. They scream for the parents phones to play games. The mom has to hand feed the kids and such. If the kids don’t get chocolate they will start breaking things. They are a wild set of kids with no boundaries." [sr085-0070]
- "I don’t honestly know how to approach that family and be like hey we can’t hang out because your kids are just terrible or at least parent your kids so our kids don’t have to experience / learn from your kids behavior." [sr085-0072]
- "I have a 2 year who is attending daycare and the daycare staff if pretty decent and have built a rapport with my wife who is currently in bootcamp for the next two months. Im a navy vet myself but what can i do help my toddler stand up for herself or what methods can i use to teach her to take it back and other than at home repetition? she currently just throws a tantrum and falls on the floor and honestly im a little pissed but i know this is the first time she’s experiencing harmless bullying but i want to nip this in bud soon without resorting to teaching her violence this soon" [sr097-0072]

**But:** The daycare concern is not a rejection of the staff: "the daycare staff if pretty decent and have built a rapport with my wife" [sr097-0072]

