Parents of newborns through toddlers around sixteen months describe long crying spells or near-continuous fussing despite feeding, changing, soothing and checking possible discomfort. They consider colic, reflux, milk sensitivity, constipation, teething, sleep and temperament, sometimes after medical consultations or treatment. Reassurance that things improve at a particular age has often failed to match their experience, and they ask for hope, explanations, solidarity or permission to vent.

- "We have literally never had a break. It’s always something." [sr106-0107]
- "I find myself dreading the next wake up because I know that more crying is coming and it's really starting to impact my mental health." [sr039-0091]
- "We’ve tried it all. Reflux meds? Got em. Tongue tie? Procedure booked. Chiro, lactation consultant, osteopath? Seen them all." [sr044-0085]
- "At this point, I feel like she will always be this way and that it’s just her personality. Can someone give me some hope?" [sr019-0116]
- "In the moments when she is not crying it’s soooo great but once the noise starts it’s like a siren going off that just does not stop." [sr016-0065]

**But:** The accounts include calm periods and partial improvements. "Things did improve around 9 months for a bit because he learned to crawl and we had his belly issues figured out for a bit. But now it’s back, and includes tantrums." [sr117-0038]

