- "I've tried pick up put down and the chair method. I did Ferber for a few days but I'm fully convinced "cry it out" doesn't work on some babies and mine is one of them." [sr107-0071]
- "Because what else do I do honestly? My husband tries just as much as me and it’s the same thing for him." [sr075-0059]
- "Now my child is having meltdowns for Bluey at bed time and still not sleeping?" [sr070-0085]
- "i wanna sleep training bcs it can't be harmful than me now, but i can't start it, there is no progress at all !" [sr030-0174]
- "but my bigger concern is just how tf to make him go to sleep before 10pm." [sr124-0063]

**But:** The travel-bedsharing post describes the reverse problem: a child who sleeps well independently but gets excited when sharing. "He loves going to sleep in his bed. However, we have a trip coming up and it would be really convenient if him and I could share a bed, but every time I try, he gets super excited and doesn’t go to sleep." [sr118-0122]

Posts: 1sddwue 1sidro8 1slhoar 1tb9w7o 1tbmr3z 1tizdfz 1tqfumz 1ubh71u 1uv01nz 1ux44me 1v0dy8i 1va55t6 1vbc5h5 1vjyogg 1vl0y31 1vn249a 1vqeufa 1wdy5c2 1wgesd8 1wsfh48 1wumq1j 1ww97ap 1wzkxmu 1xdzq3p

### They feel worn down by caring and want a break or help with their reactions.

16 of 72 posts.

These posts centre on parental depletion, isolation, resentment, regret or difficulty controlling reactions, with children from around four months through two years and some ages unknown. They describe disrupted sleep, constant contact or crying, limited help, lost time for study or work, and sometimes disagreements with a partner; some seek only a place to vent. Reported responses include breaks, therapy, medication, nursery, asking for help or stepping away, while accounts also include thoughts of harming a baby, self-directed distress, difficulty feeling love and wanting to escape.

- "I find myself having thoughts about hurting her when I get frustrated and overstimulated with her when I can’t sleep and she can’t either or can’t stop crying." [sr006-0200]
- "But what I actually meant was I didn't love him unconditionally, don't get me wrong I will never physically hurt him or cause him harm in any way. I will do my job as a father and a parent and take care of him, but it won't be out of love or selflessness." [sr127-0004]
- "When I don’t see her, I don’t miss her for example. I rather cook and clean than play with her." [sr103-0170]
- "My mom got a PHD while working and raising four children, so why the fuck am I struggling this much being a SAHM while earning a bachelor’s degree?" [sr012-0048]
- "I am starting to scream at walls under my breath & throw things (literally stuffed animals) when my son’s not around. I need help." [sr053-0091]
- "please be mindful that I am already feeling like I want to run away or worse." [sr128-0065]

**But:** Thoughts of harm are not the same as reported acts; the four-month-old's mother explicitly says she has not hurt the child. "I have never hurt her but there has been a couple times where I’ve yelled at her to be quiet and then I felt horrible about it afterwards." [sr006-0204]

Posts: 1sats5p 1skd4ll 1sn324c 1snz03v 1tdghil 1tmsod8 1to94ll 1u4fk8h 1u8l1sw 1urvomw 1vdvone 1vhv3c9 1w27zuy 1w8lqum 1x2swpj 1x5l1l7

### They want a way to get through tantrums without giving in or making the upset worse.

5 of 72 posts.

These posts describe tantrums in newly two-year-old or age-unknown children and ask how to hold a limit, help the child calm down or practise less harmful responses. They include successful redirection after holding a boundary, a parent sharing a tantrum game from an article, and requests for alternatives to spanking. Another parent links a sudden change after the second birthday with lost naps and difficulty waiting.

- "it took 20 mins of crying, everyone was asking me to cave. I was adamant, she tried to find allies (she was the only kid)" [sr002-0131]
- "they want us to put him in time out but it doesn’t feel like he understands it and it’s not teaching him anything… does anyone have resources or a book I could ready maybe!" [sr002-0229]
- "So the real advice here is to practice when he's *not* having a tantrum." [sr016-0154]
- "I want to respectfully gentle parent her without spanking her like my dad immediately tells me to do. But I also don’t want to gentle parent her where she continues this behavior thinking it’s ok." [sr075-0174]

**But:** Two contributors report improvement or success; the tantrum-game description also draws on second-hand professional advice from an article. "When we've done it the winning the game is it's own reward. Usually that's enough of a positive to snap him out of the emotion and even when it's not he's not doing his ear rattling scream so it's way more manageable." [sr016-0160]

Posts: 1sc5ex6 1se70ia 1syb93f 1tyixib 1v3u864

### They want help with sharing, jealousy or personal boundaries between children.

5 of 72 posts.

These posts describe sharing refusals, attachment to a daycare toy, sibling conflict and being on the receiving end of daycare bites; children are described as two years old or have unknown ages. Parents try modelling, books, conversations, purchasing a duplicate toy, or managing which siblings go out together. They want practical help or solidarity while distinguishing a behaviour called developmentally appropriate from repeated injury or exhausting family interactions.

- "We try and talk about taking turns or playing together and she would rather leave the activity all together than share." [sr063-0018]
- "I'm now worried she won't understand the one at daycare isn't hers and will continue to not share with the other kids. What would you do?" [sr074-0052]
- "BUT… the frequency is starting to get concerning. Today’s bite broke skin and left a big bruise." [sr032-0111]
- "I feel like it's the 3 of us on a raft in the middle of the ocean. We have food and entertainment but they're arguing and there's no where for me to go to decompress." [sr026-0117]
- "You can do all the right things as a parent, but the math is against you." [sr107-0040]

**But:** Difficulty sharing at home or community activities does not necessarily extend to daycare. "She is in daycare and social at daycare. No concerns. They say they’re working on sharing and they’re not worried." [sr063-0020]

Posts: 1tc2pr0 1ti5yy1 1ujck9i 1uzjnwh 1wev7up

### They want to know when and how a young child can learn a limit.

3 of 72 posts.

These posts ask how to get a child of unknown age or a two-year-old to cooperate or learn a limit. One parent tries numerous disciplinary responses without lasting effect, while two share or question playful challenges: doing a familiar task wrong, or pretending not to believe the child can do it. The accounts include concern about encouragement, potty refusal and whether immediate compliance means the rule has been learned.

- "Smack her hand, put her in the corner, make her sit down, take away her toys, talk to her calmly, scold her.. I mean I've tried it all." [sr119-0022]
- "He is a sensitive kid, and very prone to getting upset when he tries something and can't succeed. At the same time, he's asking me to basically neg him." [sr018-0082]
- "I've found that when my kids refuse to do something they know how to do, like putting on their clothes or putting toys away, and they're really being contrarian about it, all I have to do is do it wrong." [sr004-0191]
- "But it's like now that she knows how to do it, she doesn't want to use the potty anymore. She just wants her diaper." [sr119-0023]

**But:** The playful technique has reported success but is not presented as universally effective. "It doesn't work all the time but if your kid is giving you a hard time with not doing something you want them to do, pull this technique outta your back pocket and give it a shot." [sr004-0193]

Posts: 1sf1cia 1swb41f 1wq1fr7

### They want to set limits at meals without leaving their child hungry.

3 of 72 posts.

One parent of an eleven-month-old feels guilty replacing rejected meals with snacks, another of a two-year-old wants consequences for throwing that preserve eating and utensil practice, and a third child's age is unknown. Parents have tried asking for the plate, ignoring, suction plates, alternative foods or repeated instruction. Their questions balance food intake, learning, cleanup and whether stopping a meal would reward leaving the table.

- "making meals just feels so discouraging when it all ends up on the floor 😩 but then I feel absolutely guilty for giving her “snacks” it skipping a meal time because she just ain’t having it." [sr069-0056]
- "I could not give her utensils, but I do want her to learn how to use them (she’s pretty good aside from throwing them)." [sr018-0136]
- "We've tried ignoring it completely and not reacting. We've tried suction cup plates (he learned out to unsuction them immediately)." [sr134-0060]

**But:** One parent reports less throwing in public and partial success when asking for the plate. "We're tried preemptively asking him to hand us his plate when he's finished (it works about 30% of the time)." [sr134-0060]

Posts: 1swp7ef 1ut4wcy 1xc4tog

### They question how they could manage another baby when their first child needs so much.

2 of 72 posts.

A father with a toddler of unknown age asks how to cope with a second child, while a mother of a nineteen-month-old considers whether the time is right. Both describe the demands of the first child, and the mother also fears another episode of postpartum depression and an exhausting toddler-and-newborn combination. They seek other parents' experiences rather than reporting that a second child has already made things worse.

- "If my math is correct, 1 is stressful already, 2 doesn’t equal less stress." [sr005-0103]
- "But the other part of me feels like there’s no way I can manage a newborn and a toddler who still can’t regulate her emotions." [sr113-0148]
- "I worry that I’ll think it’s time then we’ll have the baby and I will regret not waiting longer." [sr113-0150]

**But:** The mother also reports enjoying motherhood now and sees a possible benefit in already having experience. "Part of me feels like now that not everything is brand new to me (changing diapers, birth, breastfeeding, etc) it will be easier the second time around." [sr113-0148]

Posts: 1sc9cvj 1wlv5lb

### They want to help their child through harder behaviour after a major change.

2 of 72 posts.

One parent of an age-unknown toddler reports worsening behaviour around a newborn sibling's arrival, despite daily time together without the baby. Another reports a two-year-old's prolonged screaming and disturbed sleep after removing the pacifier, with water, food, touch and stuffed toys failing to substitute. The first asks for tips or validation, and the second for another source of comfort.

- "Our son has been showing some behavior regression as the baby got closer and closer to being born. But, since she’s arrived, he’s been a terror." [sr012-0121]
- "Managing the toddler has been more difficult than the newborn by a factor of 10 it feels." [sr012-0122]
- "It has been a total shit show.  She has had horrible naps and will wake up screaming in the middle of the night for hours." [sr133-0002]
- "She tosses any stuffy we give her. We are hardcore struggling mentally here. Any advice very welcomed." [sr133-0004]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1sq2va9 1x9ol3q

### They want to stop their child hurting them or someone else, including during play.

2 of 72 posts.

Both posts describe two-year-olds, kept in the near section as supplied: one is rough with a newborn sibling and the other is expelled from daycare after biting and an unsafe hug. Parents describe modelling gentle touch, separating, planning with staff, reading and coaching interactions without the result they want. Their concerns include safety while caring alone, learning how strong the child is, and paying for care that ends before the parent thinks the child had time to adjust.

- "I can tell she isn't trying to hurt her but she absolutely doesn't even realize her own strength even after modeling what gentle means for a month." [sr019-0060]
- "How do I completely seperate two children that I have to take care of all the time by myself?" [sr019-0070]
- "Today he attempted to hug a kid from behind, however it turned into more of a choking incident and they said this compiled with the biting attempts they had to disenroll." [sr083-0085]
- "I just also feel robbed of all this money when I tried to implement some form of plan ahead of time and was assured they would implement something, all to be told that they are stretching their teachers thin to have to monitor him after barely two weeks." [sr083-0085]

**But:** One parent reports affection and remorse after accidental injuries, rather than describing an intention to harm. "She always says sorry and tries to hug after." [sr019-0057]

Posts: 1stwu0k 1vfr9o3

### They need to keep a constantly moving or exploring child safe.

2 of 72 posts.

A parent of a nineteen-month-old asks about preventing crib climbing during disturbed sleep, while another reports a two-year-old running across a road. One has comforted the child and reversed the sleep sack and plans to lower the crib again; the other asks how to teach road safety after a frightening escape. Neither post establishes that verbal teaching alone has solved the safety problem.

- "He stood back up, took off his sleep sack and started lifting his legs to climb out of the crib!" [sr010-0070]
- "We are not ready to switch to a toddler bed. I would love to keep him in the crib til 3!!" [sr010-0071]
- "I was leaving the gym and my youngest(2) ran and crossed the street. Thank god there were no cars! It could have wrong and horrible! I feel like a horrible mom:(" [sr062-0014]

**But:** The crib incident did end with successful settling on that occasion. "I ran in, picked him up, calmed him, put his sleep sack on backwards, laid him back down and he fell asleep after a bit more protesting." [sr010-0070]

