### They want to leave home or travel without the outing becoming a battle.

1 of 72 posts.

One parent on a first family holiday with a twenty-month-old worries about eating, running away, illness, injury and screaming. The money spent increases the pressure to have a good time, and the parent asks for reassurance that everyone will be okay. This is a single holiday account rather than evidence that every outing is difficult.

- "My husband planned a vacation to our favorite place (first trip as parents). I’m trying to be happy and grateful but I am so stressed over my 20mo old’s… everything?" [sr027-0016]
- "I am so worried and so worried about how much this all cost to not make it a good time." [sr027-0017]
- "I am sick to my stomach and want to cry with worry about her getting sick, hurt, screaming and crying…" [sr027-0018]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1t6bb9u

### They need relief from their child's repeated loud screaming or screeching.

1 of 72 posts.

One parent describes a newly two-year-old who has started screaming in response to questions despite knowing words, and now insists on being held while the adult stands. Outings and enjoyable activities have not reliably helped across about two weeks. The parent asks for reassurance that it is temporary and reports trying a day without television.

- "My son just turned 2 and is suddenly screaming instead of saying anything. Evey question you ask he just screams." [sr084-0053]
- "I pick him up and he just wants me to hold him. I cant even sit down and snuggle him. I have to stand." [sr084-0054]
- "This has been going on for maybe two weeks now and im losing my mind." [sr084-0055]

**But:** The same parent reports early improvement after trying a day without television. "Tried no TV today and its so far going better!" [sr084-0057]

Posts: 1vd4zpk

### They want repeated questions or demands to stop before their patience runs out.

2 of 72 posts.

Two parents of children whose ages are unknown describe repeated why questions or requests that become louder until answered. One has taught polite requests and tries responding immediately but cannot always do so while caring for a baby and cleaning a senior cat's accidents. The other says a partner took over at bedtime before the speaker snapped; both want relief from the repetition.

- "He can have more milk of course, he just needs to wait. Me telling him that he needs to wait doesn’t stop him from continuing to repeat his demand." [sr005-0175]
- "It’s just the repetition of the same phrase over and over, like a broken/scratched record, that is boiling my brain and sending steam out of my ears." [sr005-0177]
- "My husband had to take over before I snapped." [sr043-0074]

**But:** The parent of the child making repeated requests says polite wording is already understood; repetition is not evidence that the child cannot formulate a request. "So we aren’t just giving in to every stated want, and we do require him to ask politely before he gets something." [sr005-0177]

