### They want clean teeth without having to force the toothbrush into their child's mouth.

6 of 210 posts.

Parents of babies around ten months through toddlers of twenty months describe closed mouths, biting, screaming, wriggling and uncertainty that teeth are being cleaned. They try brushes, toothpaste, mirrors, joint brushing, songs or letting the child start, and some pin the child down or hold the mouth open. They want effective daily brushing without a brutal routine or lasting negative associations.

- "I can't see majority of the time and im hoping im making contact with her teeth." [sr012-0001]
- "The only way I can get them brushed is it lay her down and hold her arms down and fight with her." [sr003-0166]
- "Every morning and night I would have to wrap her in a towel like a cat and hold her head still to brush and I was terrified I was creating a lifelong trauma with toothbrushing but her teeth needed to be brushed so I did what I had to." [sr002-0009]
- "The only thing that helps a little is letting her guide the toothbrush by herself, which she obviously does a piss poor job at." [sr091-0147]

**But:** One twenty-month-old begins accepting brushing without an identifiable new method. "I can’t think of anything that we did to warrant the change - she just grew out of it." [sr002-0010]

Posts: 1s99jg9 1sf0awl 1sfbhsi 1slqcx6 1t3pv6l 1vtoouz

### They want to set limits at meals without leaving their child hungry.

9 of 210 posts.

From around one year to almost two, parents describe thrown food, bottles or utensils, rejected meals, demands for more or demands for food from an adult's identical plate. They ask whether removing food teaches a limit or simply deprives a still-hungry child, and try replacement foods, discard bowls, verbal rules or ending meals. Weight, constipation, family disagreements and wanting to eat outside the home complicate the decision.

- "if i take it away the first time he does it, he will be having 1 bite of his meal 3 times a day???" [sr114-0057]
- "I know it’s developmentally normal but am I supposed to serve her something else or is mealtime over and we try again later?" [sr046-0005]
- "My husband seems to think that this is ‘giving in’ and letting him ‘win’ and if we keep giving him whatever he wants after refusing a food, he’ll take advantage of that and just keep doing it." [sr081-0080]
- "He is very thin already and I just want to get more food in him and be able to go on vacation somewhere without planning on eating everything in the hotel room." [sr125-0036]
- "We don’t want to be overfeeding her to avoid tantrums." [sr117-0102]

**But:** Some responses help briefly or some of the time. "i tried putting an empty bowl on his table, and that helped, he would put the rest of his peice in there sometimes." [sr114-0058]

Posts: 1t1whlk 1tyg3i6 1uvrgim 1vfklrn 1vxd4sy 1wkym57 1wsn5bc 1wvsza1 1wylzhh

### They need relief from their child's repeated loud screaming or screeching.

6 of 210 posts.

Parents of children about seven months through the eighteen-to-twenty-four-month stage focus on the volume and repetition of screams, whether happy experimentation, seeking attention, demanding something or reacting to a refusal. They describe sensory strain, anxiety about neighbours or public looks, and in one case tinnitus. Toys, pacifiers, holding, outings, quiet voices, ignoring and brief separation do not consistently reduce the noise.

- "he's not upset, just finding his voice with his happy noises. but it's almost constant the entire time he's awake." [sr010-0135]
- "But it is a deafening scream and he really doesn’t listen to anything when it’s happening." [sr067-0104]
- "I have developed tinnitus in my right ear from all the high pitched screaming." [sr097-0004]
- "I'm a very reserved and introverted person, and the looks everyone give me give me horrible anxiety. I hate it so much." [sr017-0241]

**But:** One parent reports quiet independent play and good behaviour with grandparents alongside the screaming. "He can be quiet and content as well, he plays by himself and behaves really well when we visit his grandparents." [sr102-0003]

Posts: 1sk92qp 1sy1cfr 1ux5v90 1vu65e4 1vw9mzw 1w2otsw

### They want to help their child through harder behaviour after a major change.

6 of 210 posts.

Parents of children around thirteen to twenty-three months connect increased tantrums or clinging with a move, a new sibling, starting daycare or weaning. They try familiar objects, preparation, predictable routines and different soothing approaches while asking how to remain patient through the adjustment. The posts describe difficult transitions at home as well as difficulty leaving or returning from childcare.

- "Since our move a month ago I’ve noticed more and more tantrums." [sr024-0017]
- "We tried to prepare him for the move by saying, "we will be in a new house" and "you will have a new teacher soon" but he obviously didn't respond." [sr067-0022]
- "My 16 m/o is struggling with the transition and sometimes it feels impossible to console her." [sr054-0035]
- "I’ve tried all the tactics to try to regulate, explain, don’t explain and it just blows up no matter the angle I attempt." [sr069-0003]
- "I know he is going through so many changes but the nonstop crying if he isn’t on me is going to make me spiral." [sr069-0074]

**But:** Distress after childcare does not necessarily mean the child is unhappy throughout the day there. "I love his daycare provider and he seems to do fine and enjoy himself when I’m not there!" [sr087-0128]

Posts: 1t00aeo 1u6relp 1uqsoe9 1us2py7 1utijtq 1vnq1t3

### They feel worn down by caring and want a break or help with their reactions.

4 of 210 posts.

Parents of toddlers around eighteen to twenty-three months describe trying to stay patient through repeated demands or tantrums while feeling unable to take a break. Accounts range from silently swallowing frustration and stepping away to cry through shaking with stress and regretting yelling or spanking. A father who left a red mark describes breaking his commitment against physical punishment, his child calling him frightening and booking therapy.

- "My wife and I decided from the start that we would not use physical punishment. I crossed the line. I snapped and I flew off the handle and I regret my actions more than I can explain and now I don't know where to go from here." [sr030-0020]
- "He told her, "Daddy spooky". I am heartbroken and I am at a loss for words." [sr030-0021]
- "But, my God. I do not know how to keep this patience up. I can’t retreat because she follows." [sr112-0014]
- "today I put her in her bedroom and went downstairs and just sobbed." [sr116-0051]
- "Bath time was so bad the other night that I could feel myself physically shaking by the end of it because he was screaming like someone was k!lling him the entire time and wanted to get out, but once I took him out he decided that he wanted back in and that started the tantrum completely over." [sr127-0063]

**But:** Feeling near the limit does not mean every parent reports lashing out; one says the child would not know about the frustration. "And I’ve stayed patient. Even if she was older I genuinely don’t think she’d even know that I am feeling frustrated and annoyed." [sr112-0014]

Posts: 1t607d8 1wi527a 1wqa6fw 1x4dpdb

### They want to stop their child hurting their own head or pulling out their own hair.

4 of 210 posts.

These posts concern children around fourteen to twenty-two months banging their heads, hitting themselves or pulling hair, sometimes leaving bruises, bumps or a visibly thin patch. Parents try blocking, holding, comforting, cushioning, redirection or alternative movements and ask what helps rather than merely restarting the behaviour. One speaker is a nanny who also asks how to tell the parents; another post combines self-hitting with biting during play and nursing.

- "She doesn’t care if it’s grass hardwood or concrete. She will do it anywhere from 3-10 times in a row." [sr033-0013]
- "I always try to gently grab her hands and redirect her, but she will usually restart the moment I let go." [sr017-0209]
- "He has been pulling on his hair but I didn’t notice that he was pulling it out until I noticed a large spot on the top of his head that was extremely thin." [sr117-0135]
- "the head hitting i feel is some sort of self-soothing thing in a way but i definitely don’t think it’s an efficient one 🤦‍♀️" [sr118-0086]

**But:** The parent in the biting-and-head-hitting post says ending a feed sometimes passes without another upset. "sometimes he’s fine and just goes off and play, other times he will get frustrated and do the head banging again." [sr118-0085]

Posts: 1swwf3a 1tdi6ab 1wr04qa 1wu45xq

### They question the stories other people tell about what babies and toddlers are like.

4 of 210 posts.

These posts challenge gender stereotypes, frightening videos of toddler aggression, warnings that age three is worse and idealised motherhood videos. Children described range from six or nine months to almost two years; the worry may concern a future stage rather than current aggression. One lengthy, overtly humorous rant asks for honest portrayals of everyday chaos and simultaneous love and exhaustion; its exaggerations are kept as rhetoric, not treated as measured events.

- "But it made me think about how people always stereotype little girls as being calm/chill/easier than boys when honestly toddler temperament seems way more personality-based than gender-based." [sr028-0101]
- "Then everyone I talk to says that age 3 is so much harder. How will I manage for the next two years?!" [sr035-0045]
- "I have a huge fear of toddlerhood due to seeing so many videos online of toddlers being mean / hurtful.." [sr035-0118]
- "I'm saying some of us are living an entirely different genre of motherhood." [sr133-0135]

**But:** The humorous rant does not claim every calm parenting portrayal is false. "I'm not saying your peaceful little routines aren't real." [sr133-0135]

Posts: 1tanez8 1tf4nqs 1tihc2g 1xec7s4

### They need help when tiredness or the approach of sleep brings screaming and difficult behaviour.

3 of 210 posts.

One parent describes a five-month-old screaming after the bedtime bottle despite moving the feeding earlier; another connects late-day tantrums, biting and head-banging with an eighteen-to-twenty-four-month toddler refusing naps. A third post is a short hypothetical request for coping with an almost-two-year-old in a terrible mood two hours before bedtime. These posts ask what helps or whether the pattern will end, rather than all reporting the same sleep problem.

- "She takes her bottle, finishes it, screams, and then passes out." [sr034-0036]
- "So this leads to around 4pm the tantrums start. Crying biting hitting and bashing her head into things to the point where she gets bruises and bumps." [sr053-0079]
- "Imagine your almost 2 year old is in the worst mood imaginable and you still have 2 hours til bedtime-wyd?" [sr079-0019]

**But:** The bedtime-bottle post reports good overnight sleep despite the screaming before it. "Luckily she sleeps through the night." [sr034-0036]

Posts: 1thiss4 1u87xvf 1v4x86m

### They question how they could manage another baby when their first child needs so much.

2 of 210 posts.

Two parents describe difficult, sensitive or very attached first children aged twelve and nineteen months and ask about adding a sibling. One weighs an unexpected pregnancy after deciding to stop at one, while the other seeks experiences before deciding. Constant contact, distress at separation and limited time or energy for themselves shape their questions.

