# Digest 09: pattern 3, Hard behaviour

Share: baby-development/_work/2026-11/digests/share-09.md. Yes posts: 210. Near posts: 71.

## Pattern 3, share 09: yes posts

### The parent wants a way through hours of evening crying and an idea of when it will end.

6 of 210 posts.

These six posts describe evening stretches of crying that parents struggle to soothe, from an almost-three-week-old to a 15-week-old. Parents try movement, songs, nursing, gripe water, dietary changes or assessments, and ask for either a useful remedy or an end date. Several contrast this baby with an older child who did not cry this way.

- "Is there something that worked for your kiddo? I’m starting to dread night time because I know I’m going to be stuck nursing a screaming baby off and on for hours. My first never did this." [sr016-0086]
- "Of course neither of our methods worked. And it made feel like a superstitious gambler trying to bring back his hot streak with some whacky ritual that’s sure to win big one day…." [sr003-0100]
- "Was LO on a different formula before or breastmilk? I EBF and tried cutting dairy and soy from my diet for a month, but it didn’t help much." [sr021-0002]

**But:** The crying is not equally severe every night: "Some nights it's just a quick cry and he's more easily settled." [sr090-0063]

Posts: 1sdlhyz 1swruna 1syp4hd 1ursaus 1vkdr8w 1wyoefw

### The parent cannot keep their baby content for long and wants relief or an explanation.

13 of 210 posts.

These posts describe babies from newborn age to seven months who are unhappy through much of the day, with soothing working briefly or not at all. Parents describe reflux, colic, gas, allergies, constipation or an unexplained change, alongside failed feeding, carrying and sleep adjustments. The consequences include housebound days, dread of wake windows, fear of failing the baby and, in one post, an urge to leave and never return.

- "He also has to be held 24/7 and never allows us to sit. We have to hold him and walk him to keep him even a bit happy. We can’t go out because he’s hates the car and screams when we’re in public. My husband and I have to take turns eating dinner, and whichever one of us is watching him usually don’t get to eat anything all day until dinner." [sr074-0006]
- "It hurts my soul that pretty much every interaction I have with my daughter results in her screaming in my face. It feels silly to say, but I legitimately fear interacting with her at this point because I know what it is inevitably going to result in." [sr048-0042]
- "My baby will be 4 weeks tomorrow. Over the last couple of days he has become inconsolable. He cries for hours and is only happy if he is being fed or help upright. He has been refusing naps. He has barely napped over the last two days. Idk what to do anymore and have spent the majority of the day crying. I have the urge to get in the car and never come back." [sr091-0077]
- "hes been grabbing his head and ears for so i always blame teething but surely it isnt for 3 months as thats how long its been? plus he gets at least one dose of calpol or ibuprofen a day at this point we well as anbesol so i feel like i’m mothering poorly lol. like i feel like i just constantly live to distract him from crying without ever figuring out what it actually is hes trying to tell me from crying. hes 7 months and i’m obviously a first time mum. someone reassure me or give me advice😅🫠" [sr119-0054]

**But:** One follow-up reports longer happy stretches after addressing discomfort and seeing motor progress: "We have managed to help him feel better from the gas mostly, and are taking steps with the reflux, so we get much longer stretches of awake happy time." [sr071-0044]

