### The parent wants painful hitting, biting, grabbing or rough touch directed at them to stop.

3 of 71 posts.

These posts describe two-year-olds hurting parents through hitting, scratching, pinching, kicking or biting. One parent says the child scratches to convey hunger or a full nappy despite otherwise communicating well; others describe failed calm scripts, blocking, punishment or behavioural-therapy suggestions. They want an effective response without becoming a punching bag, worsening the problem or losing control.

- "I’m also noticing that I’m getting more dysregulated as this goes on. The calm “I won’t let you hit, hitting hurts” script isn’t working anymore. I don’t want to yell. I don’t want to react out of fear. I don’t want to accidentally reinforce the behavior. But I also don’t want to be a punching bag." [sr040-0106]
- "I don’t want to “bite him back” or spank him preferably (I’ve tried that-didnt work and just made me feel like a pos parent)." [sr080-0097]
- "She is good in communication ,but when she has to convey messages about being hungry ,or her diaper is full she would scratch us .We set boundaries when we say no to scratching and say what she need by holding her hands and asking what she wants ." [sr033-0109]

**But:** Timeout can stop the immediate episode without preventing a later one: "It does stop the behavior in that moment but idk if he learns anything from it, because he just hits me again later that day." [sr080-0099]

