Parents of newborns through toddlers around sixteen months describe long crying spells or near-continuous fussing despite feeding, changing, soothing and checking possible discomfort. They consider colic, reflux, milk sensitivity, constipation, teething, sleep and temperament, sometimes after medical consultations or treatment. Reassurance that things improve at a particular age has often failed to match their experience, and they ask for hope, explanations, solidarity or permission to vent.

- "We have literally never had a break. It’s always something." [sr106-0107]
