### They want help with sharing, jealousy or personal boundaries between children.

10 of 210 posts.

These posts concern children from eleven months into the eighteen-to-twenty-four-month stage: keeping toys or food, turn-taking, jealous aggression, sibling clashes, unwanted affection and distress when another child takes or hits. Parents ask how to teach care for others without expecting understanding the child does not yet have, and try modelling, redirection or separating children. One affectionate toddler kisses unfamiliar babies; another parent asks how to support the child being slapped rather than the child doing it.

- "I would like her to be a good human that is thoughtful and caring about others but I don’t think we’re heading in that direction." [sr007-0064]
- "The problem is, whatever intervention we use literally only works for about 5 seconds." [sr133-0015]
- "I hope she isnt acting this way because I dont give her enough attention?" [sr074-0037]
- "He gets upset when other people touch me, sometimes to the point of crying and attempting to pull their hands off, get in between, push them away, and has even bitten." [sr090-0106]
- "BUT how on earth do I nip this in the bud, without discouraging him from giving kisses to me or his Dad? I don’t want him to feel like he can’t show us affection." [sr080-0029]
- "To console a child, take his toy back?" [sr085-0008]

**But:** The problem can be limited to a particular activity rather than occurring everywhere. "She isn’t in daycare but we go to a lot of parent and me activities and so far she’s only had issues at the children’s gym." [sr007-0062]

